---
import StandardPageLayout from "../../layouts/StandardPageLayout.astro";
import FinancialStatementKey from "../../components/FinancialStatementKey.astro";
import Glossary from "../../components/Glossary.astro";
import PageToc from "../../components/PageToc.astro";
import "../../styles/standards-poster.css";
import "../../styles/extended.css";
import HistoryTimeline from "../../components/HistoryTimeline.astro";
import history from "../../data/history.json";
import metadata from "../../data/standards/usgaap/asc808.json";
---

<StandardPageLayout
  code={metadata.code}
  standardType={metadata.framework}
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued={metadata.issued}
  latestAmendment={metadata.latestAmendment}
  status={metadata.status}
  summary={metadata.summary}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope — Two Defining Characteristics" },
        { id: "s3", label: "Active Participation by Both Parties" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Exposure to Significant Risks and Rewards" },
        { id: "s5", label: "Income Statement Presentation — Not Automatically Revenue" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Interaction With ASC 606" },
        { id: "s7", label: "Worked Example — Cost/Profit-Sharing Presentation" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Disclosure Requirements" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Critical Judgments & Estimates" },
        { id: "s10", label: "ASC 808 vs IFRS — No Equivalent Named Category" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Related Topics" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Quick-Reference Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">

    <div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">A middle ground between a customer relationship and a joint venture</div><div class="principle">Some arrangements between two parties — common in pharmaceutical and biotech co-development deals — involve GENUINE joint operating activity and SHARED commercial risk, but WITHOUT forming a separate legal entity and WITHOUT one party simply being a "customer" of the other. ASC 808 exists to give this genuinely middle-ground arrangement its OWN accounting home, rather than forcing it awkwardly into either revenue recognition (ASC 606) or joint venture/consolidation guidance (ASC 810/323).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope — Two Defining Characteristics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A collaborative arrangement, precisely defined</div><div class="principle">ASC 808 applies to arrangements involving a JOINT OPERATING ACTIVITY where: (a) the parties are ACTIVE PARTICIPANTS in the activity, and (b) the parties are EXPOSED to SIGNIFICANT RISKS AND REWARDS dependent on the activity's COMMERCIAL SUCCESS — without the arrangement being conducted through a SEPARATE legal entity that one party CONTROLS (which would instead trigger consolidation under ASC 810).</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>Active Participation by Both Parties</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Not a passive investment or a simple vendor relationship</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">"Active participation" means BOTH parties are genuinely INVOLVED in operating decisions — this could include serving on a joint steering committee, sharing in the execution of research or commercialisation activities, or having approval rights over key strategic decisions. This distinguishes a collaborative arrangement from a PASSIVE investment, and from an ordinary vendor-customer relationship where only one party operates the activity.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Exposure to Significant Risks and Rewards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Genuine commercial risk sharing</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Both parties must be exposed to SIGNIFICANT risks and rewards that depend on whether the underlying activity is COMMERCIALLY SUCCESSFUL — for example, a pharmaceutical co-development arrangement where both parties share in R&D costs AND in eventual product profits (or losses), rather than one party simply receiving a fixed fee regardless of the product's ultimate success.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>Income Statement Presentation — Not Automatically Revenue</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A nuanced classification question</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Payments BETWEEN collaborative arrangement participants are generally NOT within ASC 606's scope UNLESS the counterparty is genuinely acting as a "CUSTOMER" for a distinct good or service (see Section 6). Where NOT a customer relationship, the income statement CLASSIFICATION of amounts exchanged (cost reimbursements, profit-sharing payments) is presented based on ANALOGY to other GAAP or a REASONABLE, RATIONAL, and CONSISTENTLY APPLIED accounting policy — often presented as a REDUCTION of the relevant expense category (e.g. netting a cost-sharing reimbursement against R&D expense) rather than as revenue.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Interaction With ASC 606</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">A threshold question determining the accounting model</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">Some COMPONENTS of a broader collaborative arrangement MAY still fall within ASC 606's scope — for instance, if one party is genuinely acting as a CUSTOMER for a specific, distinct licence or service the other party provides. Assessing whether a given payment stream reflects a customer relationship (governed by ASC 606) or a genuine risk-and-reward sharing arrangement (governed by ASC 808's presentation guidance instead) is often the KEY analytical question in applying this Topic.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>Worked Example — Cost/Profit-Sharing Presentation</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">A pharmaceutical co-development arrangement</div>
      <div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: 50/50 R&D cost sharing with profit split on approval</div><div class="wk-body">Two pharmaceutical companies jointly develop a drug candidate, sharing R&D costs 50/50 and agreeing to split eventual product profits 50/50 if approved, with both companies actively participating on a joint development committee. Company A incurs $10,000,000 of R&D costs during the year and receives a $5,000,000 reimbursement from Company B (its 50% share).</div><div class="wk-answer">Company A recognises the FULL $10,000,000 of R&D expense, and presents the $5,000,000 REIMBURSEMENT received from Company B as a REDUCTION of R&D expense (net R&D expense of $5,000,000) — NOT as revenue, since Company B is not acting as a "customer" here but as a genuine risk-sharing collaboration partner.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASC 808 requires</div><div class="check-list"><span>Nature and purpose of the collaborative arrangement</span><span>Rights and obligations of the entity under the arrangement</span><span>Accounting policy for classifying payments between participants, including the income statement classification chosen and why</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Critical Judgments & Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement matters most</div><div style="font-size:11px;color:var(--color-text-secondary);line-height:1.6;">The central judgement is determining whether an arrangement genuinely meets BOTH the "active participation" and "significant shared risk/reward" criteria — and, once confirmed as a collaborative arrangement, selecting a REASONABLE, RATIONAL, and CONSISTENT income statement presentation policy for the payments exchanged, since ASC 808 does not prescribe one specific required classification.</div></div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>ASC 808 vs IFRS — No Equivalent Named Category</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison — ASC 808 vs IFRS</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">US GAAP (ASC 808)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IFRS equivalent</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Dedicated middle-ground Topic</span><span>Yes — a specifically named category</span><span>NO equivalent named category — IFRS has no dedicated "collaborative arrangements" standard</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>How the same fact pattern would be analysed</span><span>Under ASC 808's specific presentation guidance</span><span>Either as a JOINT ARRANGEMENT under IFRS 11 (if a joint operation/venture structure exists), or under IFRS 15's general customer-relationship principles — without a distinct third category bridging the two</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="note">This is one of the clearest examples in the Codification of a genuinely US-specific organisational choice — carving out a dedicated middle-ground category for a common real-world fact pattern (particularly in pharma/biotech) that IFRS instead forces into its existing joint-arrangement or customer-relationship frameworks.</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every ASC 808 event lands</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Cost-sharing reimbursement received</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Typically presented as a reduction of the related expense, not revenue</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Profit-sharing payment</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span></span><span>Classified per the entity's reasonable, consistent accounting policy</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Related Topics</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cd"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
      <div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Topic</span><span>Connection</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 606</span><span>Applies instead where a party is genuinely acting as a customer for a distinct good/service within the broader arrangement</span></div>
      <div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 323</span><span>Applies instead if the arrangement is conducted through a separate legal entity with significant influence</span></div>
      <div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASC 810</span><span>Applies instead if one party controls a separate legal entity formed for the collaboration</span></div>
      </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
    <div class="grid">
      <div class="card cn span2">
      <div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Collaborative arrangement</div><div class="glossary-def">A contractual arrangement involving joint operating activity, active participation by both parties, and exposure to significant risks and rewards dependent on commercial success, without a controlled separate legal entity.</div></div>
      <div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Joint operating activity</div><div class="glossary-def">An activity conducted jointly by two or more parties without necessarily forming a separate legal entity, the core concept underlying a collaborative arrangement.</div></div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.ASC808 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>
